  "name": "pairwise_pref_loss_v1_stabilized",
  "intuition": "The original code had a syntax error due to improper line continuation. This version corrects that by using a clean Python function definition without line continuations. Also, it stabilizes the loss by clamping the advantage gap and cost_gap signals between reasonable bounds (-20,20) to avoid extreme numerical instability in logsigmoid. This prevents overflow/underflow in logsigmoid while keeping the preference signals meaningful. Combining the two signals additively encourages the model to prefer pairs with higher advantage and lower cost_gap. Multiplying by weight and averaging produces a scalar loss.",
  "code": "def generated_loss(batch, model_output, extra):\n    advantage_w = batch[\"advantage_w\"]\n    advantage_l = batch[\"advantage_l\"]\n    cost_gap = batch[\"cost_gap\"]\n    weight = batch[\"weight\"]\n    # Clamp inputs to logsigmoid to stabilize numerical behavior\n    adv_diff = ops.clamp(ops.sub(advantage_w, advantage_l), -20.0, 20.0)\n    cost_neg = ops.clamp(ops.neg(cost_gap), -20.0, 20.0)\n    pref_signal = ops.logsigmoid(adv_diff)\n    cost_signal = ops.logsigmoid(cost_neg)\n    combined = ops.add(pref_signal, cost_signal)\n    weighted = ops.mul(combined, weight)\n    loss = ops.mean(weighted)\n    return loss",
